# A comma sequence in parentheses parses in TypeScript, as it does in
# JavaScript. The first arm of the old parenthesized_expression committed on
# `(a` and its `)` then failed on the comma, so `(a, b)` was a syntax error.
# A single expression and an annotated one keep their trees: the hidden
# expression_sequence collapses to its one child.
<<<grammar
std.typescript
<<<input
x = (a, b);
y = (a);
z = (document: any).selection;
<<<assert
exit 0
out-absent ERROR
out-count 1 expression_sequence
out-contains type: type_annotation
